# Data licence — read this before reusing anything in derived/

**This repository does not and cannot license the data it derives.**

## What the sources say

Six sources, three different postures, none of them a CC-BY-4.0 grant:

```
3x  PeeringDB      "see https://www.peeringdb.com/ terms -- VERIFY before
                    redistributing derived data commercially"
2x  AWS            "public AWS endpoint; see https://aws.amazon.com/service-terms/"
1x  Microsoft      "publishes this openly for firewall configuration"
```

AWS service terms are a contract governing use of the service, not an open
licence. Microsoft publishing an IP range list for firewall configuration is a
statement of purpose, not a rights grant. And the PeeringDB entries carry an
explicit **VERIFY before redistributing derived data commercially** flag that
this repository set itself and has never resolved.

**That flag remains open.** Do not treat the derived PeeringDB rows as cleared
for commercial redistribution.

## Why not CC-BY-4.0

This file previously carried the verbatim CC-BY-4.0 text. Applying CC-BY-4.0
asserts that this repository holds rights it can grant onward. It does not.
**The absence of a stated restriction upstream is not a grant of rights** — it
is silence, and silence cannot be relicensed.

What this repository can say is narrower and true: the capture adds a retrieval
timestamp and a parse. Neither creates a new right over the underlying values.

